Kept the C# version direct. It records the sign separately and stores the magnitude in a `long`. That makes the boundary test easy to read, especially because the negative limit has one more allowed unit than the positive limit.

```csharp
public class Solution
{
    public int MyAtoi(string input)
    {
        const long Maximum = 2147483647L;
        const long Minimum = -2147483648L;

        int index = 0;
        int sign = 1;
        long number = 0;

        while (index < input.Length && input[index] == ' ')
        {
            index++;
        }

        if (index < input.Length && (input[index] == '+' || input[index] == '-'))
        {
            if (input[index] == '-')
            {
                sign = -1;
            }
            index++;
        }

        while (index < input.Length && input[index] >= '0' && input[index] <= '9')
        {
            number = number * 10 + (input[index] - '0');
            long signedNumber = number * sign;

            if (signedNumber > Maximum)
            {
                return (int)Maximum;
            }
            if (signedNumber < Minimum)
            {
                return (int)Minimum;
            }

            index++;
        }

        return (int)(number * sign);
    }
}
```

An important edge case is a sign followed immediately by a letter, such as `"+abc"`. No digit is read, so the result stays `0`. Leading spaces are accepted, but spaces after the first digit stop the conversion.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

A wider numeric type is not needed if the next digit is checked before it is added. The current positive magnitude can safely grow while it is at most `214748364`, and the last digit decides whether the boundary is crossed. For a negative result, the allowed magnitude is one larger.

The code does not change the input string.

```java
class Solution {
    public int myAtoi(String input) {
        int index = 0;
        int length = input.length();
        int sign = 1;
        int value = 0;

        while (index < length && input.charAt(index) == ' ') {
            index++;
        }

        if (index < length && (input.charAt(index) == '+' || input.charAt(index) == '-')) {
            if (input.charAt(index) == '-') {
                sign = -1;
            }
            index++;
        }

        while (index < length) {
            char current = input.charAt(index);
            if (current < '0' || current > '9') {
                break;
            }

            int digit = current - '0';
            int limitLastDigit = sign == 1 ? 7 : 8;

            if (value > 214748364 ||
                (value == 214748364 && digit > limitLastDigit)) {
                return sign == 1 ? Integer.MAX_VALUE : Integer.MIN_VALUE;
            }

            value = value * 10 + digit;
            index++;
        }

        return sign == 1 ? value : -value;
    }
}
```

Dry run for `"-2147483648"`: the magnitude reaches `214748364`, and the final digit is `8`. Since the sign is negative, that value is allowed, so the returned result is `-2147483648`. For `"2147483648"`, the final digit is too large for a positive result and the method returns `2147483647`.

Time: `O(n)` because the characters are checked once.
Space: `O(1)`.

The parsing order matters here. First skip spaces, then read one optional sign, and only after that accept consecutive digits. A `long long` keeps the temporary value safe while it is being built. Once the signed value leaves the 32-bit range, the answer is clamped.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int myAtoi(string input) {
        const long long INT_MAX_VALUE = 2147483647LL;
        const long long INT_MIN_VALUE = -2147483648LL;

        int index = 0;
        int length = static_cast<int>(input.size());
        int sign = 1;
        long long value = 0;

        while (index < length && input[index] == ' ') {
            index++;
        }

        if (index < length && (input[index] == '+' || input[index] == '-')) {
            if (input[index] == '-') {
                sign = -1;
            }
            index++;
        }

        while (index < length && input[index] >= '0' && input[index] <= '9') {
            value = value * 10 + (input[index] - '0');
            long long signedValue = value * sign;

            if (signedValue > INT_MAX_VALUE) {
                return static_cast<int>(INT_MAX_VALUE);
            }
            if (signedValue < INT_MIN_VALUE) {
                return static_cast<int>(INT_MIN_VALUE);
            }

            index++;
        }

        return static_cast<int>(value * sign);
    }
};
```

The scan stops at the first non-digit, so input such as `"123abc"` returns `123`. Empty input, spaces only, and a sign without digits all return `0`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The JavaScript implementation avoids `parseInt`, because `parseInt` can make the stopping rules less obvious. Each character is checked directly. Since the result is stopped at the 32-bit limit before it grows too far, the number remains safe for JavaScript arithmetic.

```javascript
class Solution {
    myAtoi(input) {
        const maximum = 2147483647;
        const minimum = -2147483648;
        let index = 0;
        let sign = 1;
        let value = 0;

        while (index < input.length && input[index] === ' ') {
            index++;
        }

        if (index < input.length && (input[index] === '+' || input[index] === '-')) {
            if (input[index] === '-') {
                sign = -1;
            }
            index++;
        }

        while (index < input.length) {
            const code = input.charCodeAt(index);
            if (code < 48 || code > 57) {
                break;
            }

            const digit = code - 48;
            const lastAllowedDigit = sign === 1 ? 7 : 8;

            if (value > 214748364 ||
                (value === 214748364 && digit > lastAllowedDigit)) {
                return sign === 1 ? maximum : minimum;
            }

            value = value * 10 + digit;
            index++;
        }

        return sign * value;
    }
}
```

Dry run with `"4193 with words"`: the digits update the value as `4`, `41`, `419`, and `4193`. The next character is a space, so the loop ends and returns `4193`. With an empty string or only spaces, the index reaches the end and the stored value remains `0`.

The input is read only, so it is not modified.

Time complexity is `O(n)`, and extra space is `O(1)`.

