This approach follows the same logic as the brute-force method but adds overflow checks during digit parsing instead of computing afterwards.

Before computing result, we must check overflow conditions.


## Approach
1. At first we are checking if the input string is empty , if it is empty then exit early.
2. If leading white spaces are found, then continue the iteration incrementing i value.
3. Instead of writing 2 conditional statements we are using 1 conditional statement and a ternary operator. `sign = (input[i] == '-') ? - 1 : 1`;
4. Inside the while loop when we encounter digits we first check for overflow conditions before computing the result.
5. Reason why we don't compute underflow condition is that result will never have -ve value while the iteration is going on.
6. Based on sign we return min / max value.
7. Reason we wrote `result > (int.MaxValue - digit) / 10`, is because later we will multiply result with 10 and then add digit to it. This is basic mathematical inEquality.
8. At last based on sign, return the postive/negative result. 

## Solution
```csharp[]
public class Solution
{
    public int MyAtoi(string input)
    {
        //Exit early
        if(string.IsNullOrEmpty(input))
            return 0;

        int n = input.Length;
        int i = 0;

        //If white space found continue with the iteration
        while (i < n && input[i] == ' ')
            i++;

        //Handle the sign 
        int sign = 1;
        if (i < n && (input[i] == '-' || input[i] == '+'))
        {
            sign = (input[i] == '-') ? - 1 : 1;
            i++; 
        }

        int result = 0;
        //Continue iteration from i     
        while (i < n && char.IsDigit(input[i]))
        {
            int digit = input[i] - '0';
            //Check for over flow before calculating result
            //Here result while iterating will never have -ve values, so only checking for over flow and according to sign returning min value.
            if (result > (int.MaxValue - digit) / 10)
            {
                return (sign == 1) ? int.MaxValue : int.MinValue;
            }
            result = result * 10 + digit;
            i++;
        }

        //After iteration is over return the result as per sign
        return result * sign;       
    }
}
```

